  legalFrameworkIntro: 'UK education law varies by nation. England (largest — where London, Manchester, Birmingham, Leeds, Bristol sit) operates Section 7 Education Act 1996 (Elective Home Education) and Section 19 Education Act 1996 (local authority alternative provision duty). Scotland operates Section 30 Education (Scotland) Act 1980. Wales and Northern Ireland have similar frameworks.',
  legalFramework: [
    { h: 'England Section 7 Education Act 1996 — Elective Home Education (EHE)', p: 'Parents responsible for ensuring children receive "suitable education" — no specific curriculum required, no registration required to begin (though Schools Bill 2024 proposed statutory registration, status may vary in 2026). Local authority can make informal enquiries. Smartious meets "suitable education" standard as accredited international curriculum provider.' },
    { h: 'England Section 19 Education Act 1996 — Alternative Provision',        p: 'Local authorities have STATUTORY duty to arrange education for children unable to attend mainstream school (medical, behavioural, exclusion, refuser). Smartious can serve these students directly (family funds) or potentially via local authority Alternative Provision commissioning arrangements (LA funds).' },
    { h: 'Scotland Section 30 Education (Scotland) Act 1980',                    p: 'Home education permitted; council permission required if withdrawing from a Scottish school. Smartious meets Scottish adequacy standard.' },
    { h: 'Wales and Northern Ireland',                                            p: 'Similar frameworks to England — parental responsibility for education, no registration to begin, local authority enquiry powers.' },
  ],

